course: dictionary
lesson: 3427
title: Extra: more than usual
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Extra means more than usual, or more than you need. «Doing extra does not speed healing» tells you that doing more exercises does not make the arm heal faster. Look for the usual amount first, then the extra on top.
explain: Extra can also be a noun. An extra is something added on, often for more money, like «extra cheese» on a pizza. In a film, an extra is a person standing in the background, not one of the stars.
explain: Extra can mean very, as in «take extra care near water». Put your meaning into the sentence to check it: «take more care near water» still makes sense, so extra means more here.
fact: Extra means more than usual, or more than needed.
fact: An extra is something added on, often for more money.
fact: An extra can also be a person in the background of a film.
fact: In maths, the total is the usual amount plus the extra: 12 sweets and 5 extra make 17 sweets.
fact: «Extra time» in a match is time added on after the usual time has run out.
exercise: The pizza came with «extra cheese». What is extra here? | Cheese that has gone off | A tin of cheese | A cheese with no flavour | *More cheese than usual, added on top
exercise: The match was tied, so the teams played «extra time». What does extra time mean? | *Time added on top of the usual time | Time lost to the rain | Time for a snack | A short break
exercise: Sam had 12 sweets and was given 5 extra. How many sweets does he have now? | 12 | *17 | 60 | 7
test: Which word means more than usual? | Ancient | *Extra | Glum | Barely
test: «Doing extra does not speed healing.» What does doing extra mean here? | Doing the exercises very slowly | Stopping all the exercises | Doing nothing at all | *Doing more of the exercises than usual
exam: Which sentence means the same as «take extra care near water»? | Take the care away from the water | Take care of a pet near water | *Take more care than usual near water | Take less care near water
